  "worked_solution": "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({x}^{5})$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{1}{(-x)^{2}}sin\\,((-x)^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(-{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({x}^{5})$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
